;; Docs for text/csv.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "csv.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "CSV, as RFC 4180 has it: read one row at a time or all at once, and written.")
  (see parse-csv csv-fold csv-for-each call-with-csv-reader csv->string write-csv CsvSource)
  (example """
    (import (text csv))

    (parse-csv "name,age\nAda,36\n")    ; (Ok [#iarray["name" "age"] #iarray["Ada" "36"]])
    (parse-csv (csv-file "data.tsv") #:separator #\tab)
    (csv-fold (csv-file "big.csv") 0 (fun (row n) (+ n 1)))   ; the number of rows
    (csv->string [["a" "b,c"] ["d \"e\"" ""]])  ; "a,\"b,c\"\n\"d \"\"e\"\"\",\n"
    """)
  (reference """
    Three layers for reading, each built on the one below:
    @code{csv-next!}, a pull reader as OCaml's @code{Csv.next} has it;
    @code{csv-fold} and @code{csv-for-each}, which hold one row at a time;
    and @code{parse-csv}, the whole document. A row is an
    @code{(IArray string)}: one array, and no object of its own, which is
    the cheapest row there is. The writer takes a row as any
    @code{Iterable} of strings, so a row read is written as it is.

    Every reader takes a source, anything with a @code{CsvSource}
    implementation, and the same options.

    Records of your own, read by the header's column names and written
    back, are @code{(text csv-codec)}'s: its @code{def/csv-type} declares a
    record and how it reads from a row.

    @strong{Options.}

    @read-table{
    | keyword             | default          | what it does |
    |---------------------+------------------+--------------|
    | @code{#:separator}  | @code{#\,}       | the character between fields |
    | @code{#:quote-char} | @code{(Some #\")} | the quote; @code{None} reads every field as it is |
    | @code{#:strict?}    | @code{#t}        | @code{#f} accepts quotes in the wrong place |
    }

    The separator and the quote have to be ASCII characters that do not end
    a line, and different from each other. Anything else raises an
    @code{ArgumentException}.

    @strong{What is read.} A row ends at LF, CR LF or CR alone, or at the
    end of the input. A field that starts with the quote ends at the next
    quote that is not doubled. In it, a doubled quote is one quote, and the
    separator and line ends are part of the text. A blank line is not a row,
    and a line end after the last row does not make an empty row. A byte
    order mark at the start is skipped. Rows can have different numbers of
    fields.

    @strong{Strict and not.} When strict, which is the default, these are
    errors: a quote in a field that does not start with one, something
    other than the separator or a line end after a closing quote, and the
    end of the input inside quotes. With @code{#:strict? #f}, as Python's
    @code{csv} reads: the quote in the first case is part of the field; in
    the second, the text up to the next separator is added to the field; in
    the third, the field ends with the input.

    @strong{Encodings.} The input is UTF-8, and a field with bytes that are
    not is an error. To read another encoding, put the port through
    @code{reencode-input-port} from @code{(std ports)} first.

    @strong{Errors.} What is wrong with the input is a @code{CsvError}, in
    a @code{Result}, with its line, row and field. An exception your own
    function raises passes through as it was. A reader streams, so one that
    fails at a row has already given every row before it.

    @strong{Writing.} A field is written in quotes only when it holds the
    separator, the quote, CR or LF, or when it is the only field of its row
    and empty, as @code{""}. So every row reads back as it was written,
    except a row with no fields, which is written as an empty line.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Errors
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc CsvError
  (summary "What is wrong with the input, and where.")
  (field message "What is wrong.")
  (field line "The line where the error is, from 1. For a quoted field that is not closed, the line where it starts.")
  (field row "The row, from 1. Blank lines are not rows.")
  (field field "The field in that row, from 1.")
  (reference """
    @code{displayed} writes all of it on one line:
    @code{line 3, row 2, field 4: a quote inside a field that does not start with one}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Sources
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc CsvInput
  (summary "What a source is read as: what csv-input answers.")
  (see CsvSource csv-input)
  (reference """
    Opaque. A type of your own gets one by handing @code{csv-input}
    something that already has a @code{CsvSource} implementation.
    """))

(:doc CsvSource
  (summary "What can be read as CSV.")
  (tparam %s "The source's type.")
  (see csv-input csv-file)
  (example """
    (impl (CsvSource Upload)
      (defun (csv-input u) (csv-input (upload-port u))))
    """)
  (reference """
    These have an implementation:

    @read-table{
    | @code{string}          | the CSV itself |
    | @code{InputPort}       | read from where the port is, a chunk at a time; left open |
    | @code{(csv-file path)} | opened through the @code{FS} effect and closed again |
    }

    A port you hand over stays open: whoever opens a resource closes it.
    """))

(:doc csv-input
  (summary "A source as the reader takes it.")
  (arg source "The source.")
  (returns "What it reads as.")
  (see CsvSource))

(:doc CsvFile
  (summary "A path to read CSV from, as opposed to a string of CSV.")
  (see csv-file))

(:doc csv-file
  (summary "A file to read, as a source.")
  (arg path "The file's path.")
  (returns "A source the reader opens, through the FS effect, and closes again.")
  (see CsvSource)
  (example """
    (parse-csv (csv-file "data.csv"))
    """)
  (reference """
    Nothing is opened until a reader reads it. A file that cannot be opened
    then raises, as @code{file-read-text} does; it is not a
    @code{CsvError}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Pulling rows
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc CsvReader
  (summary "An input being read, row by row.")
  (see call-with-csv-reader csv-next!))

(:doc call-with-csv-reader
  (summary "Calls a function with a reader that gives the rows of an input one at a time.")
  (arg source "What to read.")
  (arg proc "Called with the reader. A file is closed when it returns.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:strict? "Whether a quote in the wrong place is an error.")
  (returns "What proc answers.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed.")
  (raises System.IO.IOException "When the source is a csv-file that cannot be opened.")
  (see csv-next! csv-fold)
  (example """
    ;; The first row, and nothing after it is read.
    (call-with-csv-reader (csv-file "data.csv") csv-next!)

    (call-with-csv-reader port
      (fun (r)
        (match (csv-next! r)
          ((Ok (Some row)) ...)
          ((Ok None) ...)                     ; the end of the input
          ((Err e) ...))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The bottom layer. The reader is for @code{proc}'s use only. It reads
    from the port only as far as the rows it gives need, a chunk at a time.
    """))

(:doc csv-next!
  (summary "The next row of an input.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (returns "Some row, None at the end of the input, or the error. After an error, the same error again.")
  (see call-with-csv-reader csv-row-line csv-row-number))

(:doc csv-row-line
  (summary "The line the row last read starts on.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (returns "The line, from 1. A quoted field can hold line ends, so a row can go on past it.")
  (see csv-row-number csv-next!)
  (reference """
    For a reader built on @code{csv-next!} that finds something wrong with
    a row that is good CSV, such as a field that is not a number, and wants
    to say where, as @code{(text csv-codec)} does.
    """))

(:doc csv-row-number
  (summary "How many rows have been read, the last one included.")
  (arg rd "The reader.")
  (returns "The number of the row last read, from 1, or 0 before the first. Blank lines are not rows.")
  (see csv-row-line csv-next!))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Folds
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc csv-fold
  (summary "A seed threaded through the rows of an input.")
  (arg source "What to read.")
  (arg seed "The seed to begin with.")
  (arg f "Called with each row and the seed; answers the next seed.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:strict? "Whether a quote in the wrong place is an error.")
  (returns "The final seed, or the error.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed.")
  (raises System.IO.IOException "When the source is a csv-file that cannot be opened.")
  (see csv-for-each parse-csv)
  (example """
    ;; The sum of the second column, without the header.
    (csv-fold (csv-file "sales.csv") (Tuple #t 0)
              (fun (row seed)
                (def (Tuple header? total) seed)
                (if header?
                    (Tuple #f total)
                    (Tuple #f (+ total (string->int (iarray-ref row 1)))))))
    """)
  (reference """
    One row is held at a time, so the input can be bigger than memory. The
    rows before an error have been folded when the error is answered.
    """))

(:doc csv-for-each
  (summary "Calls a function with each row of an input.")
  (arg source "What to read.")
  (arg f "Called with each row, in order.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:strict? "Whether a quote in the wrong place is an error.")
  (returns "Ok at the end of the input, or the error.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed.")
  (raises System.IO.IOException "When the source is a csv-file that cannot be opened.")
  (see csv-fold)
  (example """
    (csv-for-each (csv-file "data.csv") (fun (row) (println (iarray-ref row 0))))
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The whole document
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc parse-csv
  (summary "Reads every row of an input.")
  (arg source "What to read.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:strict? "Whether a quote in the wrong place is an error.")
  (returns "The rows, or the first error.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed.")
  (raises System.IO.IOException "When the source is a csv-file that cannot be opened.")
  (see csv-fold call-with-csv-reader)
  (example """
    (parse-csv "a,\"b,c\"\r\n\"say \"\"hi\"\"\",\n")   ; (Ok [#iarray["a" "b,c"] #iarray["say \"hi\"" ""]])
    (parse-csv "a,b\"c")                              ; (Err ...): a quote inside a field
    (parse-csv "a,b\"c" #:strict? #f)                 ; (Ok [#iarray["a" "b\"c"]])
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Writing
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc csv-row->string
  (summary "One row as CSV text, without a line end.")
  (arg row "The fields: a vec, an IArray, a list, or any other Iterable of strings.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:quote-all? "When @code{#t}, every field is in quotes.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed, or when a field needs quotes and #:quote-char is None.")
  (see csv->string write-csv-row)
  (example """
    (csv-row->string ["a" "b,c" "say \"hi\""])   ; "a,\"b,c\",\"say \"\"hi\"\"\""
    (csv-row->string [""])                       ; "\"\"", which reads back as one empty field
    """))

(:doc csv->string
  (summary "Rows as CSV text, each ended by a line end.")
  (arg rows "The rows: a vec, a list, a Seq, or anything else that iterates over rows. A row is any Iterable of strings: a vec, an IArray, a list.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:quote-all? "When @code{#t}, every field is in quotes.")
  (key #:crlf? "When @code{#t}, lines end with CR LF, as RFC 4180 says. Else with LF.")
  (returns "The text. parse-csv reads it back as the same rows, with the same options.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed, or when a field needs quotes and #:quote-char is None.")
  (see write-csv parse-csv))

(:doc write-csv-row
  (summary "Writes one row and its line end to a port.")
  (arg port "The port.")
  (arg row "The fields: a vec, an IArray, a list, or any other Iterable of strings.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:quote-all? "When @code{#t}, every field is in quotes.")
  (key #:crlf? "When @code{#t}, the line ends with CR LF. Else with LF.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed, or when a field needs quotes and #:quote-char is None.")
  (see write-csv csv-row->string)
  (reference """
    For output made a row at a time. The row is made first, and written in
    one piece.
    """))

(:doc write-csv
  (summary "Writes rows to a port, each ended by a line end.")
  (arg port "The port.")
  (arg rows "The rows: a vec, a list, a Seq, or anything else that iterates over rows. A row is any Iterable of strings: a vec, an IArray, a list.")
  (key #:separator "The character between fields.")
  (key #:quote-char "The quote, or None for no quoting.")
  (key #:quote-all? "When @code{#t}, every field is in quotes.")
  (key #:crlf? "When @code{#t}, lines end with CR LF, as RFC 4180 says. Else with LF.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the separator or the quote is not allowed, or when a field needs quotes and #:quote-char is None.")
  (see csv->string write-csv-row)
  (reference """
    The rows are read once, in order. The text goes to the port in pieces
    of about 64 KiB, so a Seq of rows is written while it is made, and the
    whole document is never in memory.
    """))
